-- Theory questions for MTH 201. Safe to run twice.
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 201$q$, $q$Advanced Mathematics I$q$, $q$Symbolic logic$q$, $q$Define a proposition. Construct the truth table of p implies q and show that it is equivalent to (not p) or q.$q$, 5, $q$A proposition is a statement that is either true or false but not both. The statement p implies q is false only when p is true and q is false. Truth table (p, q, p implies q, not p, (not p) or q): (T, T, T, F, T), (T, F, F, F, F), (F, T, T, T, T), (F, F, T, T, T). The columns for p implies q and (not p) or q are identical in every row, so the two statements are logically equivalent.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Definition of a proposition","m":1},{"t":"Truth table for p implies q has the four correct values","m":2},{"t":"Column for (not p) or q","m":1},{"t":"Concludes that the columns match so they are equivalent","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Define a proposition. Construct the truth table of p implies q and show that it is equivalent to (not p) or q.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 201$q$, $q$Advanced Mathematics I$q$, $q$Symbolic logic$q$, $q$Define a tautology. Show that (p and q) implies p is a tautology.$q$, 5, $q$A tautology is a compound statement that is true for every possible truth value of its components. For (p and q) implies p: if p and q are both true, then p and q is true and p is true, so the implication is true. If p is true and q is false, p and q is false, so the implication is true. If p is false (q either value), p and q is false, so the implication is true. The statement is true in all four cases, so it is a tautology.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Definition of a tautology","m":1},{"t":"Checks the four truth-value cases","m":3},{"t":"Concludes it is always true","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Define a tautology. Show that (p and q) implies p is a tautology.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 201$q$, $q$Advanced Mathematics I$q$, $q$Symbolic logic$q$, $q$Distinguish between inductive and deductive reasoning and give one example of each.$q$, 5, $q$Deductive reasoning starts from general statements that are accepted as true and reaches a conclusion that must follow from them. Example: all even numbers are divisible by 2, 18 is even, so 18 is divisible by 2. Inductive reasoning observes particular cases and suggests a general rule that is probable but not certain until proved. Example: 1, 4, 9, 16 are all perfect squares and their differences are odd numbers, so we guess that every difference of consecutive squares is odd, which is then proved by deduction.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Deductive: conclusion follows necessarily from premises","m":1},{"t":"Correct deductive example","m":1},{"t":"Inductive: general rule from particular cases","m":1},{"t":"Correct inductive example","m":1},{"t":"Notes that inductive results need proof","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Distinguish between inductive and deductive reasoning and give one example of each.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 201$q$, $q$Advanced Mathematics I$q$, $q$Mappings$q$, $q$Define an injective, a surjective and a bijective mapping. Show that f(x) = 3x - 5 from the real numbers to the real numbers is bijective and find its inverse.$q$, 5, $q$A mapping is injective if different inputs give different outputs, surjective if every element of the codomain is an image, and bijective if it is both. For f(x) = 3x - 5: if f(a) = f(b) then 3a - 5 = 3b - 5, so a = b, hence f is injective. For any real y, take x = (y + 5)/3, then f(x) = y, so f is surjective. Therefore f is bijective. The inverse comes from y = 3x - 5, so x = (y + 5)/3 and f^-1(x) = (x + 5)/3.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Three definitions","m":1},{"t":"Proves injective","m":1},{"t":"Proves surjective","m":1},{"t":"Concludes bijective","m":1},{"t":"Inverse (x + 5)/3","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Define an injective, a surjective and a bijective mapping. Show that f(x) = 3x - 5 from the real numbers to the real numbers is bijective and find its inverse.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 201$q$, $q$Advanced Mathematics I$q$, $q$Complex numbers$q$, $q$Find the square roots of the complex number -3 + 4i.$q$, 5, $q$Let (a + bi)^2 = -3 + 4i. Then a^2 - b^2 = -3 and 2ab = 4, so ab = 2 and b = 2/a. Substitute: a^2 - 4/a^2 = -3, so a^4 + 3a^2 - 4 = 0, which factorises as (a^2 + 4)(a^2 - 1) = 0. Since a is real, a^2 = 1 so a = 1 or a = -1. Then b = 2 or b = -2. The square roots are 1 + 2i and -1 - 2i. Check: (1 + 2i)^2 = 1 + 4i - 4 = -3 + 4i.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Sets (a + bi)^2 = -3 + 4i and equates real and imaginary parts","m":2},{"t":"Eliminates b to get a^4 + 3a^2 - 4 = 0","m":1},{"t":"Solves a = 1 or -1","m":1},{"t":"Roots 1 + 2i and -1 - 2i","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Find the square roots of the complex number -3 + 4i.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 201$q$, $q$Advanced Mathematics I$q$, $q$Vectors$q$, $q$Define the scalar (dot) product and the vector (cross) product. For a = (1, 2, 3) and b = (2, -1, 4) find a.b and a x b.$q$, 5, $q$The dot product is a.b = a1b1 + a2b2 + a3b3 = |a||b|cos(theta) and it is a scalar. The cross product is a vector perpendicular to both a and b with magnitude |a||b|sin(theta). Here a.b = 1(2) + 2(-1) + 3(4) = 2 - 2 + 12 = 12. a x b = (2(4) - 3(-1), 3(2) - 1(4), 1(-1) - 2(2)) = (8 + 3, 6 - 4, -1 - 4) = (11, 2, -5). Check: a.(a x b) = 11 + 4 - 15 = 0, so the result is perpendicular to a.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Definitions of both products","m":2},{"t":"a.b = 12","m":1},{"t":"a x b = (11, 2, -5)","m":2}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Define the scalar (dot) product and the vector (cross) product. For a = (1, 2, 3) and b = (2, -1, 4) find a.b and a x b.$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 201$q$, $q$Advanced Mathematics I$q$, $q$Vectors$q$, $q$Find the angle between the vectors a = (1, 0, 1) and b = (0, 1, 1).$q$, 5, $q$Use cos(theta) = a.b/(|a||b|). a.b = 1(0) + 0(1) + 1(1) = 1. |a| = sqrt(1 + 0 + 1) = sqrt(2) and |b| = sqrt(0 + 1 + 1) = sqrt(2). So cos(theta) = 1/(sqrt(2) x sqrt(2)) = 1/2. Therefore theta = 60 degrees.$q$, $q$[{"t":"Formula cos(theta) = a.b/(|a||b|)","m":1},{"t":"a.b = 1","m":1},{"t":"Both magnitudes sqrt(2)","m":1},{"t":"theta = 60 degrees","m":2}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Find the angle between the vectors a = (1, 0, 1) and b = (0, 1, 1).$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 201$q$, $q$Advanced Mathematics I$q$, $q$Matrices and determinants$q$, $q$Find the inverse of the matrix A = [[2, 1], [5, 3]].$q$, 5, $q$For a 2 by 2 matrix [[a, b], [c, d]] the determinant is ad - bc and the inverse is (1/det)[[d, -b], [-c, a]]. Here det A = 2(3) - 1(5) = 6 - 5 = 1, which is not zero, so the inverse exists. A^-1 = (1/1)[[3, -1], [-5, 2]] = [[3, -1], [-5, 2]]. Check: A times A^-1 = [[2(3) + 1(-5), 2(-1) + 1(2)], [5(3) + 3(-5), 5(-1) + 3(2)]] = [[1, 0], [0, 1]].$q$, $q$[{"t":"Formula for the 2 by 2 inverse","m":1},{"t":"det A = 1","m":1},{"t":"A^-1 = [[3, -1], [-5, 2]]","m":2},{"t":"Verifies by multiplication","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Find the inverse of the matrix A = [[2, 1], [5, 3]].$q$);
insert into public.theory_questions (course_code, course_title, topic, question, marks, model_answer, key_points)
select $q$MTH 201$q$, $q$Advanced Mathematics I$q$, $q$Matrices and determinants$q$, $q$Evaluate the determinant of the matrix with rows (1, 2, 3), (0, 4, 5) and (1, 0, 6). Hence solve x + y = 5 and 2x - y = 1 by Cramer's rule.$q$, 5, $q$Expanding along the first row: det = 1(4 x 6 - 5 x 0) - 2(0 x 6 - 5 x 1) + 3(0 x 0 - 4 x 1) = 1(24) - 2(-5) + 3(-4) = 24 + 10 - 12 = 22. For the system, the coefficient determinant is D = the determinant with rows (1, 1) and (2, -1) = -1 - 2 = -3. D_x = the determinant with rows (5, 1) and (1, -1) = -5 - 1 = -6 so x = -6/-3 = 2. D_y = the determinant with rows (1, 5) and (2, 1) = 1 - 10 = -9 so y = -9/-3 = 3. Check: 2 + 3 = 5 and 4 - 3 = 1.$q$, $q$[{"t":"3 by 3 determinant 22 with working","m":2},{"t":"D = -3","m":1},{"t":"x = 2","m":1},{"t":"y = 3","m":1}]$q$::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from public.theory_questions t where t.question = $q$Evaluate the determinant of the matrix with rows (1, 2, 3), (0, 4, 5) and (1, 0, 6). Hence solve x + y = 5 and 2x - y = 1 by Cramer's rule.$q$);
select course_code, count(*) as questions from public.theory_questions group by course_code order by course_code;
